1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,968
Dev runs Calloway & Finch's smaller accounts.

2
00:00:02,968 --> 00:00:07,796
His first call using Agent 2 involved two people on the client side who happened to

3
00:00:07,796 --> 00:00:08,901
share a first name.

4
00:00:08,901 --> 00:00:13,671
The agent's action item read: "Alex to send the updated schedule." Two Alexes were

5
00:00:13,671 --> 00:00:16,871
on that call, and nothing in the output said which one.

6
00:00:16,871 --> 00:00:19,663
Dev was, for the record, doing everything right.

7
00:00:19,663 --> 00:00:24,433
Marcus had approved the rollout, the template was Priya's proven copy, and Dev was

8
00:00:24,433 --> 00:00:29,320
on day one of his own trial, checking every extracted item against his memory of the

9
00:00:29,320 --> 00:00:31,239
call, exactly as C06 told him to.

10
00:00:31,239 --> 00:00:35,893
Which is the only reason the two Alexes problem was a thirty second catch at his

11
00:00:35,893 --> 00:00:40,605
desk instead of a wrong schedule chased with a client he was still building trust

12
00:00:40,605 --> 00:00:40,896
with.

13
00:00:40,896 --> 00:00:42,932
Would you have caught it on a skim?

14
00:00:42,932 --> 00:00:47,819
Honestly, maybe not: "Alex to send the updated schedule" reads as perfectly complete

15
00:00:47,819 --> 00:00:49,447
unless you were in the room.

16
00:00:49,997 --> 00:00:53,489
This is a third failure mode, distinct from both of Priya's.

17
00:00:53,489 --> 00:00:58,320
Nothing moved, the way Section 7.3's sheet moved, and nothing was invented, the way

18
00:00:58,320 --> 00:00:59,368
Section 7.5 named.

19
00:00:59,368 --> 00:01:01,696
The agent read the transcript correctly.

20
00:01:01,696 --> 00:01:06,585
It just did not know that "Alex" needed a surname to be useful, because nothing told

21
00:01:06,585 --> 00:01:08,389
it two Alexes were in the room.

22
00:01:08,389 --> 00:01:13,220
Here is the generalisation worth keeping, because it explains why Priya's six weeks

23
00:01:13,220 --> 00:01:16,712
of clean trials could not have found this in a hundred more.

24
00:01:16,712 --> 00:01:19,738
A failure mode is not a property of the agent alone.

25
00:01:19,738 --> 00:01:24,336
It is a property of the agent paired with a particular world, and Priya's world

26
00:01:24,336 --> 00:01:28,352
simply contains no colliding first names among her regular attendees.

27
00:01:28,352 --> 00:01:33,241
Same template, different client list, different exam, and your colleagues' desks are

28
00:01:33,241 --> 00:01:34,638
all different exams too.

29
00:01:34,638 --> 00:01:39,352
That is why "Priya already proved it" is a true statement that guarantees nothing

30
00:01:39,352 --> 00:01:44,067
about Dev's desk, and why the trial has to travel with the template, every single

31
00:01:44,067 --> 00:01:46,220
time, to every new world it lands in.

32
00:01:46,770 --> 00:01:51,130
If Dev had skipped his own trial because "Priya already proved this works," he would

33
00:01:51,130 --> 00:01:55,282
have shipped that ambiguous action item straight to a client relationship he was

34
00:01:55,282 --> 00:01:56,528
still building trust in.

35
00:01:56,528 --> 00:02:00,629
Picture the version of this mistake waiting inside whatever template you hand a

36
00:02:00,629 --> 00:02:04,989
colleague next, because it exists: some fact about their clients, their sheet, their

37
00:02:04,989 --> 00:02:08,207
calendar that your world never taught your briefing to handle.

38
00:02:08,207 --> 00:02:10,594
You cannot predict it, and you do not need to.

39
00:02:10,594 --> 00:02:14,747
You need them running the same first-week discipline you ran, and the discipline

40
00:02:14,747 --> 00:02:15,837
will meet it for you.

41
00:02:15,837 --> 00:02:19,989
That is what you are actually handing over in a rollout: not four files, but the

42
00:02:19,989 --> 00:02:23,415
habit that makes four files safe on a desk you know nothing about.

43
00:02:23,965 --> 00:02:26,699
Read the table by columns and it is two stories.

44
00:02:26,699 --> 00:02:31,371
Read it by rows and it is one method, doing its job twice in two different worlds,

45
00:02:31,371 --> 00:02:33,991
which is the only way a method earns the word.

46
00:02:34,541 --> 00:02:39,121
Look at the shape of the fix too, because it settles a question every team rollout

47
00:02:39,121 --> 00:02:40,462
eventually argues about.

48
00:02:40,462 --> 00:02:44,707
Dev's surname rule went into his copy only, and that is correct, not untidy.

49
00:02:44,707 --> 00:02:49,119
His briefing now knows something true about his world that is false in Priya's,

50
00:02:49,119 --> 00:02:51,521
where the extra instruction would be noise.

51
00:02:51,521 --> 00:02:56,101
Templates fork honestly as they meet different desks, the exports from Section 3.6

52
00:02:56,101 --> 00:03:00,681
were built to make that cheap, and the thing that stays shared is not the briefing

53
00:03:00,681 --> 00:03:00,961
text.

54
00:03:00,961 --> 00:03:05,652
It is the method: job description, trial, spot checks, and a failure-mode name ready

55
00:03:05,652 --> 00:03:08,110
for whatever each new world serves up first.

56
00:03:08,110 --> 00:03:12,578
One template, two different first weeks, two different real failures caught, and

57
00:03:12,578 --> 00:03:13,919
neither of them by luck.

58
00:03:13,919 --> 00:03:18,331
That is the whole argument for why Trial never becomes optional for you, or for

59
00:03:18,331 --> 00:03:22,856
anyone you hand a template to, just because someone else already proved the shape

60
00:03:22,856 --> 00:03:23,191
works.
